    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for suggestion!\r\n\r\n> File paths almost always begins with a `/`, `.`, or `\\` symbol, which is unusual in programming language syntax.\r\n> \r\n> tl;dr I think mini.completions should fallback to file completion when a `.` or `/` symbol is under the cursor.\r\n\r\nI don't think that this behavior is universally acceptable. For example, many class methods can be thought as \"starting with `.`\".\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\n'mini.completion' is designed around \"two-step\" completion: first try LSP, then fall back to some other *configurable* source. I'd suggest one of the two solutions:\r\n\r\n- Use `<C-x><C-f>` as fallback action. Like this:\r\n```lua\r\nrequire('mini.completion').setup({\r\n  fallback_action = '<C-x><C-f>`,\r\n})\r\n```\r\n- Write your own action which does what you describe. It can use `vim.fn.getline('.')` and `vim.fn.col('.')` (or similar combination of `vim.api` functions) to compute the branching condition and then `vim.api.nvim_feedkeys(..., 'n', false)` to simulate built-in completion.\r\n\r\nClosing, as this is unplanned.",
